  {
   "id": "language-muscle",
   "title": "Building Your Language Muscle",
   "subtitle": "",
   "author": "Dominik Lukeš",
   "accent": "#187060",
   "paper": "#f7f6e9",
   "ink": "#203d35",
   "masthead": "literary",
   "cover": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
   "chapterCount": 12,
   "chapter": "Step 1: Get the right attitude",
   "breadcrumb": [
    "Building Your Muscle"
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "You need to stop thinking of learning a language as learning history or biology. Going to class, taking notes, studying at home. It is much more like learning to play a musical instrument, or training for a marathon. You can only learn by doing it. A lot. Slowly building up your muscle memory and your stamina. But you have to spend time and effort on it, going to a class once a week is not enough."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "text": "Rethink what you want from your teacher"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The first thing you have to do is realise that nobody can learn for you. Just like with building muscles, you have to put in the hard work. Stop thinking about your teacher as someone who teaches you the language. Think of your teacher as someone who tells you what and how to practice. As someone who helps you set and revise your training goals and give you feedback on your practice."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Wanting a teacher to “teach you” is like wanting your personal trainer to do the exercises for you. Your teacher will tell you what to practice but you have to do the heavy lifting. You should start thinking of your teacher as your coach instead."
    }
   ],
